{title2=Differential Equations}

= a
{parent=2a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Start from $I_0(\lambda)=1/\lambda$. <Differentiation under the integral sign> gives
$$
I_n'(\lambda)=-I_{n+1}(\lambda),\qquad I_n(\lambda)=(-1)^n\frac{d^n}{d\lambda^n}\frac1\lambda.
$$
This interchange is justified locally around any $\lambda>0$ by the <dominated convergence theorem>: the differentiated integrands are bounded by an integrable <polynomial> times $e^{-\lambda x/2}$. Successive <derivatives> of $\lambda^{-1}$ give
$$
\boxed{I_n(\lambda)=\frac{n!}{\lambda^{n+1}}.}
$$
This is also the integer case of the <Gamma function> integral after scaling the integration variable.

= b
{parent=2a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the inverse <change of variables> $u=(x+y)/2$ and $v=(x-y)/2$. The <chain rule> gives the differential operators
$$
\partial_x=\tfrac12(\partial_u+\partial_v),\qquad \partial_y=\tfrac12(\partial_u-\partial_v).
$$
The <mixed partial derivatives> cancel, assuming the twice continuously differentiable solution appropriate here. Consequently $f_{xy}=(g_{uu}-g_{vv})/4$.

**The transformed equation is**
$$
\boxed{g_{uu}-g_{vv}=4.}
$$
The factors of $1/2$ belong to the inverse <Jacobian matrix>; using the forward transformation instead would give an incorrect factor.

= 3F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= Solution
{parent=3F}

The <independent and identically distributed random variables> have a <continuous probability distribution>, so any tie has probability zero. Their <joint probability density> is unchanged by a <permutation> of their indices: this is <order symmetry of independent random variables>. The $n!$ strict orderings are disjoint and exhaust the sample space apart from the zero-probability ties. Each ordering therefore has the same probability, and their probabilities sum to one.

**The probability is**
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(X_1>X_2>\cdots>X_n)=\frac1{n!}.}
$$

= 4F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= Solution
{parent=4F}

The <moment-generating function> of a <random variable> $Z$ is $m_Z(\theta)=\mathbb E[e^{\theta Z}]$, at the real values of $\theta$ for which that <expectation> is finite. For a <standard normal distribution> variable $X$, combining the exponentials in its <probability density function> yields
$$
\mathbb E[e^{\theta X^2}]=\frac1{\sqrt{2\pi}}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-(1-2\theta)x^2/2}\,dx=(1-2\theta)^{-1/2},\qquad \theta<\tfrac12.
$$
The last equality follows by scaling the <Gaussian integral>. The variables $X_i^2$ are <independent random variables>, so the <expectation> of their product factorizes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{m_Z(\theta)=\prod_{i=1}^n\mathbb E[e^{\theta X_i^2}]=(1-2\theta)^{-n/2},\qquad \theta<\tfrac12.}
$$
This is the <moment-generating function of a chi-squared distribution> with $n$ degrees of freedom. For positive $n$ the defining <integral> diverges at and above $\theta=1/2$.

= 5A
{parent=Paper 2}
