{title2=Differential Equations}

= a
{parent=5a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Away from the impulse, the <harmonic oscillator equation> has a <homogeneous solution>. Before $x=\pi/2$, the <initial conditions> select $y=\cos x$. The <jump condition for an impulse> requires continuity of $y$ and a unit jump in $y'$: a jump in $y$ would produce a derivative of the <Dirac delta distribution>, which is absent from the forcing, while integrating through the impulse gives $[y']=1$.

Just before the impulse, $y=0$ and $y'=-1$. Just afterwards, $y=0$ and $y'=0$, so uniqueness for the <harmonic oscillator equation> makes the subsequent solution identically zero. Equivalently the causal response is $H(x-\pi/2)\sin(x-\pi/2)$, with $H$ the <Heaviside step function>, and it cancels the original oscillation.

**The solution is**
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\cos x+H(x-\pi/2)\sin(x-\pi/2)=\begin{cases}\cos x,&0\leq x<\pi/2,\\0,&x\geq\pi/2.\end{cases}}
$$
The <impulse cancellation of a harmonic oscillator> produces a continuous graph with a corner at $(\pi/2,0)$, followed by a horizontal zero line.

\Image[paper-2-impulse.png]
{title=An impulse cancels the oscillator at zero displacement}

= b
{parent=5a}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

With a positive cooling rate $k$, <Newton's law of cooling> gives $T'=-k(T-T_0)$. The temperature excess $U=T-T_0$ satisfies the <first-order linear differential equation> $U'=-kU$. Applying the <initial condition> to its <exponential decay> solution gives
$$
\boxed{T(t)=T_0+(\alpha-1)T_0e^{-kt},\qquad 0\leq t<t_1.}
$$
The temperature approaches the ambient value $T_0$, rather than zero. The intended cooling situation has $T_0>0$ and $k>0$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At the first half-temperature time, the solution of <Newton's law of cooling> gives
$$
1+(\alpha-1)e^{-kt_1}=\frac\alpha2,\qquad \boxed{t_1=\frac1k\log\frac{2(\alpha-1)}{\alpha-2}.}
$$
Since $\alpha>2$, this time is positive and finite. The instantaneous temperature increase supplies the new <initial condition> $T(t_1+)=\alpha T_0/2+\beta$. Restart the same <first-order linear differential equation> at $t_1$:
$$
\boxed{T(t)=T_0+\left[\left(\frac\alpha2-1\right)T_0+\beta\right]e^{-k(t-t_1)},\qquad t>t_1.}
$$
This is <Newton cooling with an instantaneous temperature jump>. The time translation in the <exponential decay> is essential: its prefactor is the excess immediately after the jump.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Each smooth segment follows <Newton's law of cooling>: $T'=-k(T-T_0)<0$ and $T''=k^2(T-T_0)>0$. Thus the graph decreases and is convex on both sides of $t_1$. It starts at $\alpha T_0$, falls to $\alpha T_0/2$, jumps upward by $\beta$, and then decreases asymptotically to $T_0$.

**The right-hand limit at the jump is above the original temperature**, because $\beta>\alpha T_0/2$ implies $T(t_1+)>\alpha T_0$. The vertical dotted segment in the sketch denotes an instantaneous jump, not a continuous evolution through intermediate temperatures. The example uses $\alpha=4$, $\beta/T_0=3$; its qualitative shape holds for all the stipulated parameters.

\Image[paper-2-cooling.png]
{title=Cooling interrupted by an instantaneous temperature increase}

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Impose the target temperature on the post-jump <exponential decay>:
$$
\left[\left(\frac\alpha2-1\right)T_0+\beta\right]e^{-k(t_2-t_1)}=(\alpha-1)T_0.
$$
Solving for the jump and eliminating $t_1$ using $e^{kt_1}=2(\alpha-1)/(\alpha-2)$ gives
$$
\boxed{\beta=(\alpha-1)T_0e^{k(t_2-t_1)}-\frac{\alpha-2}{2}T_0=\frac{\alpha-2}{2}T_0\bigl(e^{kt_2}-1\bigr).}
$$
**This is the unique required temperature increase, and it satisfies the stipulated lower bound.** Indeed $t_2>t_1$ implies
$$
\beta-\frac{\alpha T_0}{2}=(\alpha-1)T_0\bigl(e^{k(t_2-t_1)}-1\bigr)>0.
$$
Thus no additional restriction on the prescribed time is needed.

= 6A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= a
{parent=6a}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the stated order of the two solutions, define their <Wronskian> by
$$
W=y_1y_2'-y_1'y_2=\det\begin{pmatrix}y_1&y_2\\y_1'&y_2'\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Taking a <derivative> cancels the two mixed products: $W'=y_1y_2''-y_1''y_2$. Substituting the <second-order linear differential equation> for each second <derivative> cancels the $q$ terms and leaves
$$
\boxed{W'+p(x)W=0,\qquad W(x)=W(x_0)\exp\left(-\int_{x_0}^xp(s)\,ds\right).}
$$
This is the <Abel identity>. On an interval with continuous coefficients, <linear independence> means $W(x_0)\ne0$; hence the <Wronskian> never vanishes anywhere on that interval.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The defining <Wronskian> identity gives the <first-order linear differential equation>
$$
\boxed{y_2'-\frac{y_1'}{y_1}y_2=\frac{W}{y_1}.}
$$
Work first on an interval where $y_1\ne0$. Dividing by $y_1$ recognizes a <derivative>:
$$
\left(\frac{y_2}{y_1}\right)'=\frac{W}{y_1^2},\qquad \boxed{y_2(x)=y_1(x)\left(C+\int_{x_0}^x\frac{W(s)}{y_1(s)^2}\,ds\right).}
$$
This is <reduction of order>. Choose the nonzero <Wronskian> from the <Abel identity> to obtain <linear independence>. The constant $C$ only adds a multiple of $y_1$. If $y_1$ has zeros, this quotient formula is local; the resulting solution can be continued across ordinary zeros using the original <second-order linear differential equation>.

= b
{parent=6a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For real parameters, work on $x>0$ so arbitrary real powers are unambiguous. Taking <derivatives> gives
$$
y_1'=-\gamma x^{\gamma-1}\sin(x^\gamma),\qquad y_1''=-\gamma(\gamma-1)x^{\gamma-2}\sin(x^\gamma)-\gamma^2x^{2\gamma-2}\cos(x^\gamma).
$$
The residual after substitution is
$$
\bigl(1-a\gamma^2x^{\alpha+2\gamma-2}\bigr)\cos(x^\gamma)-\gamma\bigl(a(\gamma-1)+b\bigr)x^{\alpha+\gamma-2}\sin(x^\gamma).
$$
When $\gamma\ne0$, $x^\gamma$ ranges over $(0,\infty)$. Evaluating at the zeros of the cosine first forces the sine coefficient to vanish; then the cosine coefficient vanishes identically. If $\gamma=0$, the nonzero constant $\cos1$ cannot satisfy the equation. Thus the necessary and sufficient conditions are
$$
\boxed{\gamma\ne0,\qquad \alpha=2-2\gamma,\qquad a=\gamma^{-2},\qquad b=(1-\gamma)\gamma^{-2}.}
$$
Under these conditions the normalized coefficient is $p=(1-\gamma)/x$. The <Abel identity> gives $W=Cx^{\gamma-1}$. Choose $C=\gamma$ and apply <reduction of order> on an interval where $\cos(x^\gamma)\ne0$:
$$
y_2=\cos(x^\gamma)\int\frac{\gamma x^{\gamma-1}}{\cos^2(x^\gamma)}\,dx=\cos(x^\gamma)\tan(x^\gamma).
$$
The substitution $u=x^\gamma$ evaluates the <integral>. After continuation across the cosine zeros,
$$
\boxed{y_2(x)=\sin(x^\gamma),\qquad W(y_1,y_2)=\gamma x^{\gamma-1}\ne0.}
$$
This is a <power substitution for an oscillatory second-order equation>: in the variable $u=x^\gamma$, the equation reduces to the <harmonic oscillator equation>.

= 7A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

**Classification and characteristic exponents.** For a normalized <second-order linear differential equation>, an <ordinary point> at zero means $p$ and $q$ are <real analytic functions> there. A <regular singular point> is a singular point where $xp$ and $x^2q$ nevertheless extend as <real analytic functions> to zero. Write $p_0=\lim_{x\to0}xp(x)$ and $q_0=\lim_{x\to0}x^2q(x)$. The lowest-power coefficient in the <Frobenius method> gives the <indicial equation>
$$
\sigma(\sigma-1)+p_0\sigma+q_0=0.
$$
Its roots are the <characteristic exponents at a regular singular point>. If their difference is not an integer, there are two <linearly independent> solutions $x^{\sigma_j}A_j(x)$, with $A_j$ <real analytic> and $A_j(0)\ne0$. For distinct real roots $\sigma_1>\sigma_2$ with integer difference, a solution for the larger root exists, and a second has the form $x^{\sigma_2}B(x)+c\,y_1\log x$; the logarithmic coefficient can vanish. For a repeated root, the second solution has the form $y_1\log x+x^{\sigma_1}B(x)$ after normalization. This is a <logarithmic Frobenius solution>. Complex conjugate roots $a\pm ib$ give real solutions whose leading behaviour is $x^a\cos(b\log x)$ and $x^a\sin(b\log x)$, with analytic-series corrections. All these descriptions are on $x>0$, or on a fixed complex branch.

For the present equation, division by $x$ gives $p=1+(1-m)/x$ and $q=(1-m)/x$. Therefore $xp=x+1-m$ and $x^2q=(1-m)x$ are <real analytic>. **Zero is regular singular when $m\ne1$; when $m=1$, it is ordinary after removing the common factor $x$.** In that exceptional case the normalized equation is $y''+y'=0$.

For the <Frobenius method> substitution, the lowest coefficient and the general <linear recurrence relation> are
$$
\boxed{\sigma(\sigma-m)=0,\qquad \sigma=0\ \text{or}\ m,}
$$
$$
(n+\sigma)(n+\sigma-m)a_n+(n+\sigma-m)a_{n-1}=0\quad(n\geq1).
$$
These equations come respectively from the coefficients of $x^{\sigma-1}$ and $x^{n+\sigma-1}$. At $m=1$, the same formal exponents $0,1$ describe the two possible leading powers at an <ordinary point>, rather than singular behaviour.

= a
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the exponent $\sigma=m$, the <linear recurrence relation> reduces to $(m+n)a_n=-a_{n-1}$. The excluded nonpositive integers ensure that no denominator is zero. Normalize $a_0=1$ and iterate:
$$
a_n=\frac{(-1)^n}{(m+1)(m+2)\cdots(m+n)}.
$$
The <ratio test> gives an infinite radius of convergence for the <power series> factor. Hence a solution is
$$
\boxed{y_m(x)=x^m\left(1+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^nx^n}{(m+1)(m+2)\cdots(m+n)}\right).}
$$
For nonintegral $m$, choose instead the exponent $\sigma=0$. Since $n-m\ne0$ for every positive integer $n$, its <linear recurrence relation> gives $na_n=-a_{n-1}$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y_0(x)=e^{-x}.}
$$
The distinct nonintegral leading exponents establish <linear independence>. More explicitly, $W(e^{-x},y_m)=m e^{-x}x^{m-1}$ by the <Abel identity> and the leading coefficient at zero, so the <Wronskian> is nonzero. The factor $x^m$ uses the real branch on $x>0$.

= b
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For positive integral $m$, the exponent-zero <linear recurrence relation> fixes $a_n=(-1)^na_0/n!$ for $1\leq n<m$, but at $n=m$ its factor $n-m$ vanishes and leaves $a_m$ free. Choose $a_0=1$ and $a_m=0$. All later coefficients then vanish by the same <linear recurrence relation>.

**A polynomial solution of degree $m-1$ is**
$$
\boxed{P_{m-1}(x)=\sum_{n=0}^{m-1}\frac{(-1)^nx^n}{n!}.}
$$
For $m=1$ this is the constant solution $1$, in agreement with the <ordinary point> exception. The <terminating Frobenius series> arises because the coefficient equation at the resonant index becomes $0=0$, permitting truncation rather than forcing a logarithm.

= c
{parent=7a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Here the <indicial equation> has the repeated root $0$. The first <Frobenius method> solution is $y_1=e^{-x}$. Since $p=1+1/x$, the <Abel identity> gives $W=Ce^{-x}/x$. <Reduction of order> with $C=1$ produces
$$
y_2=e^{-x}\int\frac{e^x}{x}\,dx=e^{-x}\left(\log x+\sum_{j=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^j}{j\,j!}\right),\qquad x>0,
$$
where the additive constant is absorbed into $y_1$. In particular the logarithmic coefficient is nonzero: an additional independent <real analytic> solution does not exist.

**The general local form is**
$$
\boxed{y(x)=C_1A(x)+C_2\bigl(A(x)\log x+B(x)\bigr),\quad A(x)=e^{-x},\quad B\text{ analytic at }0.}
$$
This is the repeated-root <logarithmic Frobenius solution>. On the negative real side one may use $\log|x|$, or use a fixed logarithm branch for a complex solution.

= 8A
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Differential Equations}

= a
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write the system as $\mathbf x'=M\mathbf x$, with $M=\begin{pmatrix}2&5\\-1&-2\end{pmatrix}$. Its <characteristic polynomial> is $\lambda^2+1$, so its <eigenvalues> are $i$ and $-i$. An <eigenvector> for $i$ is $v=(5,-2+i)^{\mathsf T}$. Thus $e^{it}v$ is a complex solution, and its <real part> and <imaginary part> give real solutions:
$$
u(t)=\begin{pmatrix}5\cos t\\-2\cos t-\sin t\end{pmatrix},\qquad w(t)=\begin{pmatrix}5\sin t\\\cos t-2\sin t\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Their initial vectors are $(5,-2)^{\mathsf T}$ and $(0,1)^{\mathsf T}$, with determinant $5\ne0$. Consequently they are <linearly independent> and span the two-dimensional solution space.

**The general real solution is**
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}x(t)\\y(t)\end{pmatrix}=\alpha\begin{pmatrix}5\cos t\\-2\cos t-\sin t\end{pmatrix}+\beta\begin{pmatrix}5\sin t\\\cos t-2\sin t\end{pmatrix}.}
$$

= b
{parent=8a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a constant matrix, the <matrix exponential> series and its differentiated series converge uniformly on bounded time intervals, as follows by comparison with the scalar exponential in a <operator norm>. Termwise <derivatives> therefore give
$$
\frac{d}{dt}e^{tM}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{t^{n-1}M^n}{(n-1)!}=Me^{tM},\qquad e^{0M}=I.
$$
Hence $\mathbf x(t)=e^{tM}\mathbf x_0$ solves the <initial value problem>. For this particular matrix, direct multiplication gives $M^2=-I$. Splitting the <matrix exponential> into its even and odd powers proves
$$
\boxed{e^{tM}=I\cos t+M\sin t=\begin{pmatrix}\cos t+2\sin t&5\sin t\\-\sin t&\cos t-2\sin t\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
This is the <matrix exponential when the square is minus the identity>. If $\mathbf x_0=(x_0,y_0)^{\mathsf T}$, the <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{x(t)=x_0\cos t+(2x_0+5y_0)\sin t,\qquad y(t)=y_0\cos t-(x_0+2y_0)\sin t.}
$$
The constants in part (a) are $\alpha=x_0/5$ and $\beta=y_0+2x_0/5$, which makes the two forms identical.

= 9F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= Solution
{parent=9F}

Set $U=X/(X+Y)$ and $V=X+Y$. Their inverse <change of variables> is $X=UV$, $Y=(1-U)V$, with domain $0<U<1$, $V>0$. The absolute <Jacobian determinant> of this inverse transformation is
$$
\left|\det\begin{pmatrix}v&u\\-v&1-u\end{pmatrix}\right|=v.
$$
Use the <independence of random variables> to multiply the two original <Gamma distribution> densities. The <change of variables formula> then gives the <joint probability density>
$$
f_{U,V}(u,v)=\frac{\theta^{n+m}}{(n-1)!(m-1)!}\,u^{n-1}(1-u)^{m-1}v^{n+m-1}e^{-\theta v},\qquad 0<u<1,\ v>0.
$$
Factor this into two normalized densities:
$$
f_{U,V}(u,v)=\left[\frac{(n+m-1)!}{(n-1)!(m-1)!}u^{n-1}(1-u)^{m-1}\right]\left[\frac{\theta^{n+m}}{(n+m-1)!}v^{n+m-1}e^{-\theta v}\right].
$$
The support is a product domain, and the factors are precisely a <Beta distribution> density and a <Gamma distribution> density. Therefore
$$
\boxed{U\sim B(n,m),\qquad V\sim\Gamma(n+m,\theta),\qquad U\text{ and }V\text{ independent}.}
$$
This <beta-gamma independence> uses the common rate $\theta$; it is a rate parameter, not the scale parameter $1/\theta$.

= 10F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= a
{parent=10f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For any fixed bin, each ball supplies an independent <Bernoulli distribution> indicator with success probability $1/m$. Summing the $n$ <indicator variables> gives
$$
\boxed{B_i\sim\operatorname{Bin}(n,1/m).}
$$
The full occupancy vector has a <multinomial distribution>, the basic <uniform balls-in-bins allocation>. Distinct bin counts are **not independent** when $n\geq1$ and $m\geq2$. To see this, write $B_i=\sum_{r=1}^n I_{r,i}$. For the same ball, $I_{r,i}I_{r,j}=0$ if $i\ne j$, while indicators from distinct balls are independent. Their <covariance> is therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Cov}(B_i,B_j)=-\frac{n}{m^2}<0\quad(i\ne j).}
$$
With no balls the counts are deterministic; with one bin there are no distinct bin pairs. These degenerate cases do not contradict the conclusion.

= b
{parent=10f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Express the three counts as sums of <indicator variables> for $B_i=0$, $B_i=1$ and $B_i\geq2$. The <linearity of expectation> applies even though the bin counts are dependent. The <binomial distribution> gives
$$
p_0=(1-1/m)^n,\qquad p_1=\frac nm(1-1/m)^{n-1}.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[E]=m(1-1/m)^n,\qquad \mathbb E[S]=n(1-1/m)^{n-1},}
$$
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[C]=m\left[1-(1-1/m)^n-\frac nm(1-1/m)^{n-1}\right].}
$$
The relation $E+S+C=m$ checks the sum of these <expectations>. For $m=1$, use the direct deterministic occupancies, or the usual binomial convention $0^0=1$ when $n=1$.

= c
{parent=10f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

There are more bins than balls, so the <pigeonhole principle> forces at least one empty bin. In fact $E\geq m-n=(a-1)n$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(E=0)=0.}
$$
For the expected fraction, substitute $m=an$ into part (b). The elementary exponential limit, obtained by taking logarithms, gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathbb E[E]}m=\left(1-\frac1{an}\right)^n\longrightarrow e^{-1/a}.}
$$
This is the empty-bin case of the <Poisson limit for occupancy fractions>, with limiting mean occupancy $1/a$.

= d
{parent=10f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

If $C=0$, every bin contains at most one ball, so the total number of balls is at most $m$. Since $n=dm>m$, the <pigeonhole principle> makes that event impossible:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(C=0)=0.}
$$
As $n\to\infty$ along these values, $m\to\infty$ and the mean occupancy $n/m$ is $d$. Apply part (b):
$$
\frac{\mathbb E[C]}m=1-\left(1-\frac1m\right)^{dm}-d\left(1-\frac1m\right)^{dm-1}.
$$
Both powers tend to $e^{-d}$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mathbb E[C]}m\longrightarrow1-(1+d)e^{-d}.}
$$
The <Poisson limit for occupancy fractions> interprets the answer as the probability that a <Poisson distribution> variable with mean $d$ is at least two.

= 11F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= a
{parent=11f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Write $\mu=\mathbb E[X]$ and $A=\{X>\theta\mu\}$. The finite <second moment> implies a finite first <moment>, and nonnegativity with $\mathbb E[X^2]>0$ implies $\mu>0$. On $A^c$, one has $0\leq X\leq\theta\mu$. Therefore
$$
\mathbb E[X\mathbf1_{A^c}]\leq\theta\mu\,\mathbb P(A^c)\leq\theta\mu.
$$
Subtract this from $\mathbb E[X]=\mu$ to obtain the <truncated first moment bound>:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X\mathbf1_A]\geq(1-\theta)\mathbb E[X].}
$$
The <indicator function> uses the strict event specified in the question; any mass at the threshold belongs to $A^c$.

= b
{parent=11f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For real <random variables> with finite <second moments>, the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> is
$$
\boxed{|\mathbb E[Y_1Y_2]|^2\leq\mathbb E[Y_1^2],\mathbb E[Y_2^2].}
$$
The product is integrable because $2|Y_1Y_2|\leq Y_1^2+Y_2^2$. If $\mathbb E[Y_2^2]=0$, then $Y_2=0$ almost surely and the result is immediate. Otherwise the nonnegative <expectation> of a square gives, for every real $t$,
$$
0\leq\mathbb E[(Y_1-tY_2)^2]=\mathbb E[Y_1^2]-2t\mathbb E[Y_1Y_2]+t^2\mathbb E[Y_2^2].
$$
Choose $t=\mathbb E[Y_1Y_2]/\mathbb E[Y_2^2]$. The resulting inequality is exactly the claimed <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>. Equality holds precisely when $Y_1$ is a constant multiple of $Y_2$ almost surely in this nondegenerate case.

= c
{parent=11f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Apply the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> to $Y_1=X$ and the <indicator variable> $Y_2=\mathbf1_A$. Since $\mathbf1_A^2=\mathbf1_A$, it gives
$$
\bigl(\mathbb E[X\mathbf1_A]\bigr)^2\leq\mathbb E[X^2]\,\mathbb P(A).
$$
Both sides of the <truncated first moment bound> in part (a) are nonnegative, so squaring it preserves the inequality. Combining the bounds and dividing by the positive <second moment> proves
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P\bigl(X>\theta\mathbb E[X]\bigr)\geq(1-\theta)^2\frac{\mathbb E[X]^2}{\mathbb E[X^2]}.}
$$
This is the <Paley-Zygmund inequality>. It includes $\theta=0$; at $\theta=1$ the lower bound is zero and remains valid.

= 12F
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}
{title2=Probability}

= a
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For each unordered triple of distinct <vertices>, let $I_A$ be the <indicator variable> that its three <edges> are present. This is a <binomial random graph>, so edge independence gives $\mathbb E[I_A]=p^3$. The <triangle count in a binomial random graph> is $T=\sum_A I_A$. There are $\binom n3$ such triples; all are triangles in the <complete graph>.

**The combinatorial maximum and expectation are**
$$
\boxed{T_{\max}=\binom n3,\qquad \mathbb E[T]=\binom n3p^3.}
$$
The maximum is attainable with positive probability if $p>0$; for $p=0$, the <random variable> is identically zero. The <linearity of expectation> requires no independence between different triangle indicators.

= b
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Markov inequality> states that for a nonnegative <random variable> $Z$ and $a>0$,
$$
\mathbb P(Z\geq a)\leq\frac{\mathbb E[Z]}a.
$$
Because $T$ is integer-valued, apply it with $a=1$. With $p=n^{-\alpha}$,
$$
\mathbb P(T>0)=\mathbb P(T\geq1)\leq\binom n3 n^{-3\alpha}\leq\frac16n^{3-3\alpha}\longrightarrow0\quad(\alpha>1).
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T=0)\longrightarrow1.}
$$
This is the <first moment method>: if the expected <triangle count in a binomial random graph> tends to zero, triangles occur with vanishing probability.

= c
{parent=12f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <Chebyshev inequality> states that for a <random variable> $Z$ with finite <variance> and $a>0$,
$$
\mathbb P\bigl(|Z-\mathbb E[Z]|\geq a\bigr)\leq\frac{\operatorname{Var}(Z)}{a^2}.
$$
Write $\mu_n=\mathbb E[T]$. The ratio in the hypothesis presupposes $\mu_n>0$ for all sufficiently large $n$. On the event $T=0$, the deviation from the mean equals $\mu_n$. Thus the <Chebyshev inequality> with $a=\mu_n$ gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T=0)\leq\frac{\operatorname{Var}(T)}{\mathbb E[T]^2}\longrightarrow0.}
$$
This is the <second moment method>. It uses only the stipulated relative <variance> bound; no assumption that triangle indicators are independent is needed.
